## 3. One carve-out — `js/worldmap-data.js`

That one file is **not** ours to license. It is an adaptation of a
third-party work and carries **CC BY-SA 3.0**, which is share-alike:
if you adapt it, your adaptation must stay under the same licence.
Full attribution and the open question about its scope:
[`THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md`](THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md).

Nothing else in this repository is share-alike. Do not let that one file
mislead you about the rest.

---

## Why attribution and not exclusivity

Because being cited is the point. A relocation reference earns its
authority by being quoted, checked and argued with. Someone learning from
this work, or quoting a figure and linking back, is the project working
as intended.

What these licences exist to prevent is narrower and specific: taking the
verified collection and presenting it as your own work, unattributed, so
that a reader has no way of knowing where the figures came from or who to
hold to account when one is wrong. Attribution is not vanity here — it is
how a reader traces a claim back to the sourcing behind it.

## A note on the copyright holder

The holder is named as **CanILiveThere**, the project, rather than an
individual. That is deliberate: it keeps a personal name out of a public
file, and it means the notice does not need rewriting if the project is
ever placed inside a company. If that happens, assign the copyright to
the company explicitly rather than assuming the project name carries it.

## Not legal advice

This file was drafted with care and is not a substitute for a lawyer.
Nothing here has been reviewed by one. If anything ever turns on it —
enforcement, a commercial dispute, incorporation — get proper advice
before relying on these words.
